**O(1) — Constant**:
```java
int first = arr[0];                          // array index
int size = list.size();                      // cached field
map.put(key, value);                          // HashMap put (average)
set.contains(item);                           // HashSet contains (average)
queue.offer(x);                               // ArrayDeque offer
```

**O(log n) — Logarithmic**:
```java
int idx = Arrays.binarySearch(sortedArr, target);   // binary search
map.get(key);                                        // TreeMap get (red-black tree)
queue.poll();                                        // PriorityQueue poll
```
Each step halves the search space. 20 steps suffice for n=1M.

**O(n) — Linear**:
```java
for (int x : list) { sum += x; }              // list traversal
list.contains(x);                              // ArrayList contains (linear scan)
list.indexOf(x);                               // linear scan for position
System.arraycopy(src, 0, dst, 0, n);          // n-element copy
```

**O(n log n) — Linearithmic**:
```java
Collections.sort(list);                        // merge sort (object) / Timsort
Arrays.sort(arr);                              // dual-pivot quicksort (primitive)
stream.sorted().toList();                      // backed by Arrays.sort
```
Best achievable for comparison-based sorting. Good trade-off for most cases.

**O(n²) — Quadratic**:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) { ... }       // nested loops
}

// Pairwise comparison
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    for (int j = i + 1; j < list.size(); j++) {
        if (list.get(i).equals(list.get(j))) { ... }
    }
}

// Repeated contains on a list — classic O(n²) trap
for (String s : input) {
    if (seen.contains(s)) { ... }             // O(n) each → O(n²) total
    seen.add(s);
}
// Fix: use a HashSet → O(1) each, O(n) total
```

**O(2^n) — Exponential**:
```java
// Naive recursive Fibonacci
int fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);           // branches into 2 per call
}
// fib(40) ≈ 1 billion calls

// Generate all subsets of a set
void subsets(int[] arr, int i, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> out) {
    if (i == arr.length) { out.add(new ArrayList<>(cur)); return; }
    subsets(arr, i+1, cur, out);              // exclude
    cur.add(arr[i]);
    subsets(arr, i+1, cur, out);              // include
    cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
}
// 2^n subsets
```

**O(n!) — Factorial** (beyond the big six, but occasionally appears):
```java
// Generate all permutations
// n=10: ~3.6 million; n=20: 10^18 — infeasible
```

**Walk the code mentally and count operations as a function of n**:

**Single loop**:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { // O(n)
    doConstantWork(i);
}
```

**Nested loops — same n**:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) { // O(n²)
        work();
    }
}
```

**Nested loops — different variables**:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < b.length; j++) { // O(a · b)
        work();
    }
}
```

**Loop with dependent inner loop**:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) { // inner runs 0, 1, 2, ..., n-1
        work();
    }
}
// Total: n(n-1)/2 = O(n²)
```

**Loop with halving**:
```java
for (int i = n; i > 0; i /= 2) { // O(log n)
    work();
}
```

**Loop with inner log**:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { // O(n log n)
    Arrays.binarySearch(sortedArr, value);
}
```

**Recursion**:
- Linear recursion (tail or single branch): O(n) time, O(n) stack.
- Divide by half each call: O(log n).
- Branch into 2 at each step (like Fibonacci): O(2^n).
- Divide and combine (merge sort): O(n log n) via master theorem.

**Common trap — O(n) inside O(n)**:
```java
// BAD: O(n²) because list.contains is O(n)
for (String s : list) {
    if (list.contains(s.toUpperCase())) { ... }
}

// BETTER: precompute a HashSet → O(n)
Set<String> upper = list.stream().map(String::toUpperCase).collect(toSet());
for (String s : list) {
    if (upper.contains(s.toUpperCase())) { ... }
}
```

`HashMap` uses an internal array of buckets. When too full, it **rehashes**:
```java
// Simplified HashMap internals
Entry[] table;
int size;
static final float LOAD_FACTOR = 0.75f;
int threshold;   // = table.length * LOAD_FACTOR

public V put(K key, V value) {
    if (size >= threshold) {
        resize();   // double the table, rehash all entries — O(n)
    }
    // place entry in appropriate bucket — O(1) average
    // ...
    size++;
}

private void resize() {
    Entry[] oldTable = table;
    Entry[] newTable = new Entry[oldTable.length * 2];
    // rehash every entry into the new table
    for (Entry e : oldTable) {
        int newIdx = hash(e.key) % newTable.length;
        // ... insert into newTable ...
    }
    table = newTable;
    threshold = (int) (newTable.length * LOAD_FACTOR);
}
```

Same analysis:
- Worst-case single `put`: O(n) (resize).
- Amortized per put: O(1).
- Total for n puts: O(n).

**Load factor 0.75** is a trade-off: lower = fewer collisions but more memory and more frequent resizes; higher = more collisions but better memory utilization. 0.75 is the Java default and is a good balance.

**Pre-sizing**:
```java
// If you know you'll insert ~1000 entries
Map<String, User> map = new HashMap<>((int)(1000 / 0.75f) + 1);
// Avoids resize during the 1000 inserts
```
Important for hot paths and large batch inserts.

**Real-time systems**: a trading app with p99 latency SLA cannot tolerate occasional O(n) blips from resize:
```java
// BAD for low-latency: occasional resize blocks the thread
List<Event> events = new ArrayList<>();
while (receiving) {
    events.add(nextEvent());   // amortized O(1) but worst-case O(n)
}
```
Solution: **pre-size** or use bounded structures:
```java
List<Event> events = new ArrayList<>(MAX_EVENTS);   // one allocation, no resize

// Or ring buffer for fixed-size window
ArrayDeque<Event> buffer = new ArrayDeque<>(WINDOW_SIZE);
```

**Concurrent systems**: amortized bounds assume single-threaded. Under contention, resize locks matter more than amortization:
```java
// ConcurrentHashMap uses a different strategy:
// - table is split into segments (Java 7) or fully concurrent (Java 8)
// - resize is incremental to avoid blocking all threads
```

**Memory-constrained systems**: amortization often trades time for memory. The 1.5x growth factor means average wasted space is ~25% of list size. If memory is tight, use a trimmed collection:
```java
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(knownSize);
// ... add exactly knownSize items ...
// No waste, no amortization games

// For existing lists, trim waste
((ArrayList<?>) list).trimToSize();
```

**Avoid amortization when predictability matters**:
- Pre-size collections where size is knowable.
- Use `ArrayList(initialCapacity)` and `HashMap(initialCapacity, loadFactor)`.
- Use `EnumMap`, `BitSet`, or fixed-size arrays for known-size domains.
- Profile p99/p999 latency — amortized O(1) can hide 10-100ms blips.

**Step 1**: Identify the recursive structure.
- How many sub-problems per call? (factor `a`)
- How much smaller is each sub-problem? (factor `n/b`)
- How much non-recursive work per call? (function `f(n)`)

**Step 2**: Write the recurrence:
- `T(n) = a · T(n/b) + f(n)`

**Step 3**: Solve with master theorem, recursion tree, or substitution.

**Example — Merge sort**:
```java
void mergeSort(int[] arr, int l, int r) {
    if (l >= r) return;                          // base case: O(1)
    int m = (l + r) / 2;
    mergeSort(arr, l, m);                         // T(n/2)
    mergeSort(arr, m + 1, r);                     // T(n/2)
    merge(arr, l, m, r);                          // O(n) linear merge
}
```
Recurrence: `T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + O(n)`.

**Example — Binary search**:
```java
int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target, int l, int r) {
    if (l > r) return -1;                         // O(1) base
    int m = (l + r) / 2;
    if (arr[m] == target) return m;               // O(1) compare
    if (arr[m] < target) return binarySearch(arr, target, m+1, r);  // T(n/2)
    else                return binarySearch(arr, target, l, m-1);  // T(n/2)
}
```
Recurrence: `T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1)`.

Naive recursive Fibonacci is O(2^n) because it **recomputes the same subproblems**. Two fixes:

**Memoization** (top-down DP):
```java
Map<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();

long fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);
    long result = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    memo.put(n, result);
    return result;
}
```
- Each subproblem computed **once**.
- New recurrence: T(n) = T(n-1) + O(1) [the n-2 is already memoized].
- **T(n) = O(n)**, O(n) space.

**Tabulation** (bottom-up DP):
```java
long fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    long a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        long c = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = c;
    }
    return b;
}
// O(n) time, O(1) space
```

**Matrix exponentiation**: 
- Use `[[1,1],[1,0]]^n` and fast exponentiation.
- **O(log n)** time.

**General principle**: when a recursion has **overlapping subproblems**, memoization reduces it from exponential to polynomial (usually O(n · problem-size) or O(n)).

**Problems where this matters**:
- Fibonacci, catalan numbers, binomial coefficients.
- Dynamic programming: longest common subsequence, edit distance, knapsack.
- Pathfinding, DP on trees, bitmask DP.
